# Lesson 5, new exercise: opener shows spades.
title: Partner asks, you have spades
id: L05-07
type: exercise
lesson: 5
concepts: 5.2
student: S
board: 14
dealer: E
vulnerable: none

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  AK95   974    73     A932
  E  QJ6    53     KJT95  J54
  S  T743   AQ8    A42    KQ6
  W  82     KJT62  Q86    T87

auction:
  E Pass
  S 1NT*   = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 15 points and a balanced hand: open 1NT.
  W Pass
  N 2C     = Stayman: do you have a 4-card major?
  E Pass
  S 2S*    = Four spades, not four hearts
     key: Four spades, so bid 2♠.
     not 2D: 2♦ says "no 4-card major." You have four spades.
     not 2NT: Answer partner's question. 2♠ shows your spades.
  W Pass
  N 4S     = Four spades too, and game values
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  North's 2♣ asked for a 4-card major. You showed spades, and North, who also
  has four spades and enough for game, bid 4♠. South is declarer, because
  South bid spades first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 5
  remind: What is the contract?

play:
  1   W H6  N H4  E H3  S H8*
      H8 key: The 8 is enough to win. Save the ace.
      H8 not HA: Did you see that your 8 already wins the trick? Keep the ace for later.
  2   S ST  W S2  N S5  E SJ
      note: Start on trumps.
  3   E DK  S D2  W D6  N D3
  4   E DJ  S DA  W D8  N D7
  5   S S7  W S8  N SK  E S6
      note: Back to trumps: three are still out.
  6   N SA  E SQ  S S3  W H2
      note: The queen falls. Trumps are drawn.
  7   N C2  E C4  S CK  W C7
      note: Trumps are drawn. Now take your club winners, starting with the king, a high card from the short side.
  8   S CQ  W C8  N C3  E C5
      note: And the queen.
  9   S HA  W HT  N H7  E H5
      note: Cash the ace of hearts.
  10  S D4*  W DQ  N S9  E D5
      D4 key: Ruff your last diamond loser in dummy.
      D4 not HQ: Did you count your losers? Dummy can still ruff your last diamond.
      D4 not S4: Did you count your losers? Dummy can still ruff your last diamond.
      note: Ruff a diamond in dummy.
  11  N CA  E CJ  S C6  W CT
      note: Dummy's ♣A.
  12  N C9  E D9  S HQ  W HJ
      note: Dummy's last club is a winner.
  13  N H9  E DT  S S4  W HK
      note: Declarer ruffs the last trick. 11 tricks: 4♠ made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: draw trumps, cash your winners, and ruff your last diamond loser
  in dummy. Winning trick 1 with the ♥8 saves the ace, and leading the last
  diamond sets up the ruff.

  4♠ made with an overtrick. 3NT would have gone down: the defense could set up
  West's long hearts. The 4-4 spade fit gave declarer a way to ruff and kept
  the hearts under control.
